1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,300
What’s the first thing you remember doing?
你记忆中很早发生的事是什么？

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:09,930
It could be playing with your friends at school, or going to a birthday party and eating amazing cake.
可能是在学校和朋友们玩耍，或是去参加生日派对，吃到了美味的蛋糕。

3
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:17,540
Most of us have a treasured early memory of our childhood, but can we really believe those vivid memories?
我们大多数人都有一段珍藏的童年早期回忆，但那些鲜活的记忆真的可信吗？

4
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:22,350
Did those special moments really happen – or did we make them up?
那些特别的瞬间是真实发生过，还是我们自己编造出来的？

5
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:30,400
It’s a strange concept to grasp, but according to research, about four out of 10 of us invent our first childhood memory.
这是个让人难以理解的奇怪概念，但研究显示，大约十分之四的人会虚构自己的第一段童年记忆。

6
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:44,950
Rather than having experienced something, we could have fabricated a fake memory from videos or photos we’ve seen. We could have been influenced by a story recounted to us that spurs our minds on to adopt someone else’s memory as our own.
我们或许并没有亲身经历过某件事，而是通过看过的视频或照片编造出了一段虚假的记忆；也可能是受他人讲述的故事影响，从而将别人的记忆当成了自己的。

7
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:53,840
What it means is that memories of our younger years, especially before the age of two, may be inaccurate, or entirely false.
这意味着，我们儿时的记忆，尤其是两岁之前的记忆，可能是不准确的，甚至是完全虚假的。

8
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:57,540
But why don’t we have clear memories from that age?
但为什么我们没有那个年龄段的清晰记忆呢？

9
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:03,390
Well, our ability to retain memories from before the age of two isn’t great.
原因在于，我们保留两岁前记忆的能力并不强。

10
00:01:03,390 --> 00:01:16,880
While at that age we do have short-term memories, according to Catherine Loveday, an expert in autobiographical memory at the University of Westminster, the memories that infants make are not long-lasting.
威斯敏斯特大学自传体记忆专家凯瑟琳·洛夫迪表示，婴幼儿虽然拥有短期记忆，但这些记忆并不持久。

11
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:21,910
This is possibly due to the rapid creation of brain cells in our early years.
这可能是因为我们在幼年时期，脑细胞处于快速增殖的阶段。

12
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:31,970
Some scientists also believe that as we get older, our childhood memories fade and after the age of seven, we get some kind of ‘childhood amnesia’.
一些科学家还认为，随着年龄增长，童年记忆会逐渐模糊，七岁之后，我们会出现某种程度的“童年健忘症”。

13
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:35,530
So why do we create fake memories?
那么，我们为什么会编造虚假记忆呢？

14
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:43,060
Some experts believe that there is a clear desire for a sense of self and having a cohesive story of our existence.
一些专家认为，这源于人们对自我认同感的明确渴望，以及想要构建连贯人生叙事的需求。

15
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:49,320
Creating memories can fill in the gaps – giving us a more complete structure for our early lives.
创造记忆能够填补人生的空白，让我们的成长阶段有更完整的脉络。

16
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:55,510
As we get older, we want to have a complete picture of our entire lives.
随着年龄增长，我们都希望拥有一段完整的人生图景。

17
00:01:55,510 --> 00:02:09,660
So, the next time someone says they have a clear memory from when they were one – or even before - just remember that while it could be true, there’s a chance they just invented it at some point in their lives.
所以，下次再有人说自己清晰记得一岁甚至更小的时候发生的事，你要知道，这件事有可能是真的，但也有可能是他们在人生的某个阶段编造出来的。
